  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss, steeper along x. Without noise, x diverges when the learning rate is above 0.5 (4 × rate > 2), and z never does in this range.",
    "Noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with sd 3·√((64−b)/(63b)): 3 at batch 1, exactly 0 at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "The orange arrow is the next real step, −rate × (slope + noise). The grey arrow is −rate × true slope. Both are drawn at their true length in bowl units (scale 1).",
    "Diverged: loss above 6 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. A run also ends after 40 steps. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence. A new run uses the next values.",
    "Running, the ball makes 5 updates per second at 1× speed. Heights on screen are capped at the bowl's rim, and the Loss readout keeps the true value."
  ]
}